# ASYMMETRY WITNESS for tools/check_mode_check.sh — a PERMANENT, INTENTIONAL one.
#
# `zebra -c` is front-end-only, and `--help` promises that some errors only `zig` can see.
# check_mode_check gates that promise by requiring a witness: a program the front end
# ACCEPTS and a full compile REJECTS. If no witness survives, the gate fails on purpose —
# the claim would have quietly stopped being true.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-08-04). The witness set used to be three BUG FIXTURES, and two
# of them were not demonstrating a design limit at all — they were SELFHOST GAPS. The
# bootstrap rejected both `bug099_unresolved_test` and `bug106_heterogeneous_list_test`,
# which means a Zebra front end demonstrably COULD catch them; the selfhost just had not
# been given the check. Using those as evidence that "`-c` cannot see everything" was
# circular: it proved a missing feature, not a boundary. BUG-106's check has since been
# ported, and the fixture no longer passes `-c` at all.
#
# A `zig'…'` / `zig"…"` backend literal is the honest article. Zebra passes its contents
# through to the emitted Zig UNPARSED — that is the entire point of the construct — so the
# front end cannot judge it even in principle, no matter how good the TypeChecker becomes.
# The asymmetry here can never be "fixed", which is exactly what a witness should rest on.
#
# EXPECTED: `zebra -c` exits 0. `zebra --check-full` fails inside zig.
# Do NOT "fix" this file. If it ever starts failing `-c`, the front end has begun parsing
# backend literals, which would be a language change worth discussing rather than patching.
def main()
    var x: int = zig"@compileError(\"witness: this literal is deliberately invalid Zig\")"
    print(x.toString())
